{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Who are you, my daughter?</b> It is possible that she did not see her until she opened the door for her to enter, as is the custom. And Scripture abbreviated, to say 'I am Ruth, your handmaid.' And like it: 'they called him and he ate bread,' and it is written 'and Moses consented.' And Rabbi Jonah the Grammarian said that מי = מה. And the meaning is 'what happened to you.' And he told me that like it is 'מי שמך' (Judges 13:17), for there is no rule for מי to be found concerning a matter, since it concerns a person. In my opinion, שמך is a proper noun, and therefore מי concerns the matter. [n: 'And he told me' — Ibn Ezra reports Rabbi Jonah's rejoinder; the reference is to מי שמך in Judges 13:17, where שמך is read as a name rather than 'your name']" "<b>The man did for her.</b> The oath. [n: Ibn Ezra identifies 'the man did for her' as referring to the oath, i.e., Boaz's sworn pledge to act as redeemer]"], :book :ruth, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The bracketed note about Rabbi Jonah's argument and the Judges 13:17 parallel is included in the translation itself for clarity, not added by the translator."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Ruth -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/ruth"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:43:33.904439227Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}